# OTC release manifest (issue #94).
#
# One release per line, OLDEST FIRST, tab-separated:
#
#   <version>	<script sha256 or ->	<web-dist.tar.gz sha256 or ->	<summary>
#
# A device stores the version it is on in /etc/otc/version and applies every
# release *after* that one, in order, to reach the newest. That is what
# makes an update incremental: a device two releases behind runs two
# release scripts, not a reinstall.
#
# This file is read from the main branch, so a device sees a release as
# soon as it is published here. Everything a release actually installs -
# the source it builds from, and the web assets - comes from that
# release's own GitHub Release, by tag, so a device always gets the
# artefacts that belong to the version it is moving to rather than
# whatever main happens to hold at that moment.
#
# Columns:
#
#   version   An integer, one higher than the last. This is the only
#             signal a device has that anything is new.
#   script    sha256 of scripts/updates/<version>.sh, or "-" when the
#             release needs no migration - which is most of them. The
#             checksum is verified before the script runs as root.
#   assets    sha256 of the web-dist.tar.gz attached to the release, or
#             "-" to leave the installed web app alone. Every release
#             should ship assets (see CLAUDE.md); "-" exists so a mistake
#             degrades instead of breaking the update.
#   summary   Shown in Settings, so write it for whoever is deciding
#             whether to press Update.
#
# Release scripts MUST be idempotent. A device with no /etc/otc/version -
# every install made before this feature existed - starts from the
# beginning and runs all of them, so re-running one must be a no-op rather
# than an error, and must never destroy data. Schema changes are ADD
# COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS / CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS, never a DROP and
# never a re-import of db.sql.
#
# See CLAUDE.md, "Cutting a release", for the end-to-end process.
#
